WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.207 --> 00:00:02.550
(percussive music)

00:00:02.550 --> 00:00:04.530
- [Arthur] As a drummer,
I've always been fascinated

00:00:04.530 --> 00:00:05.793
by the power of rhythm.

00:00:07.230 --> 00:00:08.970
It's the heartbeat of music,

00:00:08.970 --> 00:00:10.953
the driving force that moves us.

00:00:12.210 --> 00:00:13.980
I've spent my life exploring percussion

00:00:13.980 --> 00:00:15.090
and pushing the boundaries

00:00:15.090 --> 00:00:17.790
of what's possible with sticks and skins.

00:00:17.790 --> 00:00:19.020
But there's another kind of beat

00:00:19.020 --> 00:00:20.520
that's been around for centuries.

00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:22.380
One that's not played on drum

00:00:22.380 --> 00:00:26.039
but on a different kind of
instrument, the human body.

00:00:26.039 --> 00:00:26.872
♪ Here we go now ♪

00:00:26.872 --> 00:00:29.789
(beatboxing music)

00:00:35.460 --> 00:00:38.329
- [Linda] This is beatboxing,

00:00:38.329 --> 00:00:39.420
the art of creating rhythm and sound

00:00:39.420 --> 00:00:41.778
using only the human voice.

00:00:41.778 --> 00:00:44.695
(beatboxing music)

00:00:46.770 --> 00:00:49.230
- [Arthur] As a drummer, I'm
fascinated by this art form

00:00:49.230 --> 00:00:50.880
and how it compares to what I do.

00:00:52.620 --> 00:00:54.360
Can the human body really compare

00:00:54.360 --> 00:00:56.759
to the power of a drum kit?

00:00:56.759 --> 00:00:58.050
(drum beats)

00:00:58.050 --> 00:00:59.310
- [Linda] Arthur and I are going to meet

00:00:59.310 --> 00:01:01.500
with beat boxers from across the country

00:01:01.500 --> 00:01:03.000
to better understand the history

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:06.194
of beatboxing and its
impact on music today.

00:01:06.194 --> 00:01:09.111
(beatboxing music)

00:01:10.525 --> 00:01:12.600
(both laughing)

00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:14.280
- Alright, let's not do
that, let's not do that.

00:01:14.280 --> 00:01:15.690
- [Arthur] So I'm meeting up

00:01:15.690 --> 00:01:18.450
with Karnish, the executioner today.

00:01:18.450 --> 00:01:21.450
A beatboxing legend here in Minneapolis.

00:01:21.450 --> 00:01:22.830
Oh, he's been all over the world though,

00:01:22.830 --> 00:01:23.970
he's been touring the world.

00:01:23.970 --> 00:01:25.230
I got my drums with me today,

00:01:25.230 --> 00:01:26.550
we're gonna get in here and create.

00:01:26.550 --> 00:01:28.170
I'm very excited to work with Karnish.

00:01:28.170 --> 00:01:29.730
I've played with him a few times before

00:01:29.730 --> 00:01:31.500
at jam sessions here and there,

00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:33.354
but today is gonna be special.

00:01:33.354 --> 00:01:35.313
Oh here he is, what am I?

00:01:36.720 --> 00:01:38.013
- Good to see you, man.
- Likewise.

00:01:38.013 --> 00:01:39.987
- I've been too long bro.

00:01:39.987 --> 00:01:42.654
(beatbox music)

00:01:51.480 --> 00:01:54.450
What are some common
misconceptions about beatboxing

00:01:54.450 --> 00:01:55.500
that you run into?

00:01:55.500 --> 00:01:57.630
- One is that it's not music,

00:01:57.630 --> 00:02:01.320
a lot of them say it's making
noises with your mouth.

00:02:01.320 --> 00:02:05.236
A lot of people don't lead
first foot forward saying music.

00:02:05.236 --> 00:02:08.730
(beatbox music)

00:02:08.730 --> 00:02:13.140
I disagree, I think that
noises are musical too.

00:02:13.140 --> 00:02:15.330
You know, every instrument makes a noise

00:02:15.330 --> 00:02:17.975
and it's turned into music.
- Music.

00:02:17.975 --> 00:02:21.180
(beatbox music)

00:02:21.180 --> 00:02:23.190
- Another misconception is that,

00:02:23.190 --> 00:02:25.585
not everybody can learn how to beatbox.

00:02:25.585 --> 00:02:27.870
(beatbox music)

00:02:27.870 --> 00:02:31.110
But anybody can learn
how to turn themselves

00:02:31.110 --> 00:02:32.400
into an instrument.

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:35.190
And that's what I believe beatboxing is.

00:02:35.190 --> 00:02:38.313
It's the art of transforming
your body into an instrument.

00:02:38.313 --> 00:02:40.980
(beatbox music)

00:02:42.450 --> 00:02:43.830
- The art of creating music

00:02:43.830 --> 00:02:47.070
with your mouth, throat and
body goes back to ancient times.

00:02:47.070 --> 00:02:49.260
But the culture of
beatboxing as we know it

00:02:49.260 --> 00:02:51.900
didn't emerge until the 1970s and 80s.

00:02:51.900 --> 00:02:53.426
Back then, the word beatbox

00:02:53.426 --> 00:02:55.410
was used to describe drum machines,

00:02:55.410 --> 00:02:59.010
like the CR-7030 or the legendary TR-808.

00:02:59.010 --> 00:03:01.590
So when early pioneers
started mimicking drum sounds

00:03:01.590 --> 00:03:05.618
with their mouths, that became
known as human beatboxing.

00:03:05.618 --> 00:03:06.750
(train engine roaring)

00:03:06.750 --> 00:03:08.520
Right now we are in New York City,

00:03:08.520 --> 00:03:10.410
the birthplace of beatboxing.

00:03:10.410 --> 00:03:12.810
But we're gonna travel
across the Hudson River

00:03:12.810 --> 00:03:16.260
to Jersey City to visit the Beatbox House.

00:03:16.260 --> 00:03:17.760
A collective of beat boxers

00:03:17.760 --> 00:03:21.000
who have won multiple
world battle championships.

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:23.700
The band originated in
Brooklyn over a decade ago

00:03:23.700 --> 00:03:26.130
and is made up of five core members.

00:03:26.130 --> 00:03:27.384
Gene Shinozaki,

00:03:27.384 --> 00:03:28.217
(Jean beatboxing)

00:03:28.217 --> 00:03:29.050
Kenny Urban,

00:03:29.050 --> 00:03:29.883
(Kenny beatboxing)

00:03:29.883 --> 00:03:30.716
Napom,

00:03:30.716 --> 00:03:31.549
(Kenny beatboxing)

00:03:31.549 --> 00:03:32.382
Amit,

00:03:32.382 --> 00:03:33.215
(Kenny beatboxing)

00:03:33.215 --> 00:03:34.048
and Chris Celiz.

00:03:34.048 --> 00:03:35.100
The boundary pushing group

00:03:35.100 --> 00:03:37.590
says they aim to rebrand the art form.

00:03:37.590 --> 00:03:39.690
The notion that I have is
that you started the group.

00:03:39.690 --> 00:03:40.560
- Yes.
- Is that correct?

00:03:40.560 --> 00:03:42.240
Okay, so how did you start the group?

00:03:42.240 --> 00:03:45.000
- Basically we wanted a place where people

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:49.276
could come who would beatbox,
you know, to beatbox.

00:03:49.276 --> 00:03:51.657
(beatbox music)

00:03:51.657 --> 00:03:54.630
- You know, we would see each
other at every competition,

00:03:54.630 --> 00:03:57.540
you know, we would consistently
be in like the top five.

00:03:57.540 --> 00:04:00.990
So we were like, let's form a group.

00:04:00.990 --> 00:04:02.010
- Yeah.
- You know,

00:04:02.010 --> 00:04:03.090
you start doing this thing

00:04:03.090 --> 00:04:04.380
that's really weird just by yourself,

00:04:04.380 --> 00:04:07.878
but you know you really enjoy
it and you just keep doing it,

00:04:07.878 --> 00:04:10.078
and you want to find
other people like that.

00:04:11.380 --> 00:04:14.160
♪ What it is to live ♪

00:04:14.160 --> 00:04:15.540
- [Linda] Though, the
members of Beatbox House

00:04:15.540 --> 00:04:17.820
made their name by winning
world battle titles,

00:04:17.820 --> 00:04:19.950
they told me that their focus has changed

00:04:19.950 --> 00:04:21.390
to making original music

00:04:21.390 --> 00:04:23.340
and exporting American beatbox culture

00:04:23.340 --> 00:04:25.560
to other parts of the world.

00:04:25.560 --> 00:04:28.260
- What we're doing now is
producing music basically, right?

00:04:28.260 --> 00:04:30.120
We're making these electronic tracks,

00:04:30.120 --> 00:04:33.540
but instead of Gene picking a synth to use

00:04:33.540 --> 00:04:35.547
and pitching it we're just using,

00:04:35.547 --> 00:04:36.390
(Napom beatboxing)

00:04:36.390 --> 00:04:37.443
and then boom.

00:04:38.376 --> 00:04:39.540
(beatbox music)

00:04:39.540 --> 00:04:41.730
- [Linda] What are some misconceptions

00:04:41.730 --> 00:04:43.440
that people have about beatboxing?

00:04:43.440 --> 00:04:44.970
- That it's a party trick,

00:04:44.970 --> 00:04:46.620
that's like the biggest thing,

00:04:46.620 --> 00:04:48.180
people are like, oh,
that's a cool party trick,

00:04:48.180 --> 00:04:49.440
you do a sound and they're like,

00:04:49.440 --> 00:04:50.640
oh that's a cool party trick.

00:04:50.640 --> 00:04:53.310
But what we're doing is
trying to show people

00:04:53.310 --> 00:04:55.796
that beatboxing is music.

00:04:55.796 --> 00:04:58.629
(both beatboxing)

00:05:00.660 --> 00:05:03.720
- [Arthur] Today, beatboxing
is an international phenomenon

00:05:03.720 --> 00:05:05.430
with a global battle scene that pulls

00:05:05.430 --> 00:05:07.083
in millions of viewers online.

00:05:08.310 --> 00:05:09.930
And beatboxing can be found

00:05:09.930 --> 00:05:11.825
in the recordings of hip hop songs.

00:05:11.825 --> 00:05:14.408
(upbeat music)

00:05:17.220 --> 00:05:19.770
It's even made its way into
songs outside of hip hop

00:05:19.770 --> 00:05:21.364
like this one from Thorm Yorke.

00:05:21.364 --> 00:05:24.750
(upbeat music)

00:05:24.750 --> 00:05:26.790
So how did the art of beatboxing grow

00:05:26.790 --> 00:05:28.920
to become so influential?

00:05:28.920 --> 00:05:31.890
- It all started during
the early days of hip hop.

00:05:31.890 --> 00:05:34.950
Back then beatboxing became an
alternative to drum machines

00:05:34.950 --> 00:05:36.570
which were not very affordable.

00:05:36.570 --> 00:05:39.000
Early beat boxers learned
to mimic the sounds of drums

00:05:39.000 --> 00:05:40.710
when a drum machine wasn't available.

00:05:40.710 --> 00:05:44.130
Beat boxers accompanied MCs
on the streets during ciphers,

00:05:44.130 --> 00:05:47.283
on stage at a show, and
even in recorded songs.

00:05:47.283 --> 00:05:49.731
♪ La di da di, we like the party ♪

00:05:49.731 --> 00:05:50.734
♪ We don't cause trouble ♪

00:05:50.734 --> 00:05:52.230
♪ We don't bother nobody where ♪

00:05:52.230 --> 00:05:54.420
- [Arthur] But beatboxing
didn't stay the accompaniment,

00:05:54.420 --> 00:05:56.640
eventually it became the main attraction.

00:05:56.640 --> 00:05:59.970
Pioneers like Doug E. Fresh,
Buffy from the Fat Boys,

00:05:59.970 --> 00:06:02.880
and Bis Markie will take
beatboxing to new heights

00:06:02.880 --> 00:06:06.630
by incorporating beatboxing
both on stage and in the studio.

00:06:06.630 --> 00:06:09.840
The track "La Di Da Di" by
Doug E. Fresh and Slick Rick

00:06:09.840 --> 00:06:11.520
became an instant classic

00:06:11.520 --> 00:06:14.321
from one of the most sampled
tracks in hip hop history.

00:06:14.321 --> 00:06:16.988
(beatbox music)

00:06:18.137 --> 00:06:18.970
- How is beatboxing important

00:06:18.970 --> 00:06:21.150
to the history and culture of hip hop?

00:06:21.150 --> 00:06:25.320
- It's the instrument that
you always have on you.

00:06:25.320 --> 00:06:28.696
It's the easiest way to be
a part of hip hop culture.

00:06:28.696 --> 00:06:31.613
(Napom beatboxing)

00:06:33.150 --> 00:06:36.330
- A lot of people now say,
beatboxing isn't just hiphop

00:06:36.330 --> 00:06:38.760
it's idiom, it's dance
music, blah, blah blah,

00:06:38.760 --> 00:06:42.390
but I still feel like
beatboxing is still hip hop.

00:06:42.390 --> 00:06:45.210
Because for me, hip hop
isn't just a style of music,

00:06:45.210 --> 00:06:49.770
it's a culture, and beatboxing kind of,

00:06:49.770 --> 00:06:52.773
it's stemmed from that
culture of like, you know,

00:06:54.330 --> 00:06:56.310
people being oppressed
and trying to speak out

00:06:56.310 --> 00:06:57.740
express themselves and beatboxing

00:06:57.740 --> 00:06:59.440
is a form of expression, you know.

00:07:00.750 --> 00:07:02.850
- As the art form of beatboxing grew,

00:07:02.850 --> 00:07:04.410
more artists began contributing

00:07:04.410 --> 00:07:06.780
by innovating and creating new techniques.

00:07:06.780 --> 00:07:11.100
Artists like Kenny Muhammad,
Rahzel, Wise, D-Cross,

00:07:11.100 --> 00:07:13.320
Kid Lucky and many, many more.

00:07:13.320 --> 00:07:16.890
Kid Lucky specifically was an
important mentor and teacher

00:07:16.890 --> 00:07:19.230
for other rising beatboxers in the scene.

00:07:19.230 --> 00:07:21.690
By the nineties,
beatboxing was well known.

00:07:21.690 --> 00:07:24.270
Even Queen Latifah got
her start beatboxing

00:07:24.270 --> 00:07:26.190
for the group Ladies Fresh.

00:07:26.190 --> 00:07:28.110
- As beatboxing grew in popularity

00:07:28.110 --> 00:07:30.720
in underground battle
scene began to emerge,

00:07:30.720 --> 00:07:34.380
Kenny Muhammad and Rahzel became
stars in the battle scene,

00:07:34.380 --> 00:07:37.500
this health beatboxing
popularity spread overseas

00:07:37.500 --> 00:07:40.890
where competitions popped
up across the world.

00:07:40.890 --> 00:07:43.830
I got to meet some of the
best battlers in the world.

00:07:43.830 --> 00:07:45.540
The members of the Beatbox House

00:07:45.540 --> 00:07:47.580
all got their start by
rising through the ranks

00:07:47.580 --> 00:07:49.620
of the international battle scene.

00:07:49.620 --> 00:07:54.000
- Well, the main avenue for
beatboxing is the competition,

00:07:54.000 --> 00:07:55.470
especially with a lot of young kids,

00:07:55.470 --> 00:07:57.570
like they want to get into this thing

00:07:57.570 --> 00:07:58.980
and they see that that's the lane,

00:07:58.980 --> 00:08:00.839
so it's like, okay, let me invest my time

00:08:00.839 --> 00:08:03.300
into like learning how to do that.

00:08:03.300 --> 00:08:04.440
'Cause that's what we did,

00:08:04.440 --> 00:08:06.510
like our goal is like,
let's win world champs,

00:08:06.510 --> 00:08:10.500
like let's win grand
beatbox battle, and we did.

00:08:10.500 --> 00:08:11.907
♪ Because it's a beatbox ♪

00:08:11.907 --> 00:08:15.180
(beatbox music)

00:08:15.180 --> 00:08:17.430
- Beatboxing became a sensation online

00:08:17.430 --> 00:08:19.620
where battle videos and YouTube tutorials

00:08:19.620 --> 00:08:22.470
help bring beatboxing
to millions of new fans.

00:08:22.470 --> 00:08:24.300
As the community continues to grow,

00:08:24.300 --> 00:08:26.490
there are more and more
women taking over the scene

00:08:26.490 --> 00:08:27.720
and winning titles.

00:08:27.720 --> 00:08:30.270
like Kaila Mullady, DJ Hershe,

00:08:30.270 --> 00:08:32.769
Pe4enkata, and many more.

00:08:32.769 --> 00:08:35.686
(Napom beatboxing)

00:08:36.870 --> 00:08:38.910
Napom has dozens of battle titles.

00:08:38.910 --> 00:08:40.770
So I couldn't pass up the opportunity

00:08:40.770 --> 00:08:43.320
to ask him for a quick beatbox lesson.

00:08:43.320 --> 00:08:45.360
Okay, I'm so nervous to ask

00:08:45.360 --> 00:08:48.330
but can you teach me how to do something?

00:08:48.330 --> 00:08:49.163
- Yeah.
- Something easy,

00:08:49.163 --> 00:08:52.290
I am a singer, but like
I am not a beatboxer.

00:08:52.290 --> 00:08:53.880
- The basic one is boots and cats.

00:08:53.880 --> 00:08:55.022
- Okay.
- So there's three sounds.

00:08:55.022 --> 00:08:57.803
(Napom beatboxing)

00:08:57.803 --> 00:08:59.220
- Okay.
- So the first one is the B,

00:08:59.220 --> 00:09:00.540
you just gotta make a hard B sound.

00:09:00.540 --> 00:09:01.463
So like,

00:09:01.463 --> 00:09:02.670
(Napom beatboxing)

00:09:02.670 --> 00:09:03.811
Do that like five times.

00:09:03.811 --> 00:09:06.011
(Napom beatboxing)

00:09:06.011 --> 00:09:06.873
- It's hard.

00:09:06.873 --> 00:09:08.610
(Linda beatboxing)

00:09:08.610 --> 00:09:09.874
Yeah, precisely.
- Okay.

00:09:09.874 --> 00:09:10.884
- And then,

00:09:10.884 --> 00:09:12.991
(Napom beatboxing)

00:09:12.991 --> 00:09:13.824
So it's like a T and S together.

00:09:13.824 --> 00:09:14.657
So if you go,

00:09:15.810 --> 00:09:18.213
and then add a little, after.

00:09:20.310 --> 00:09:21.660
The last one is the K,

00:09:21.660 --> 00:09:22.980
So C where a K sound.

00:09:22.980 --> 00:09:24.563
So just,

00:09:24.563 --> 00:09:26.093
(Napom beatboxing)

00:09:26.093 --> 00:09:28.563
(Linda laughs)

00:09:28.563 --> 00:09:29.396
- Like that.
- Pretty much perfect,

00:09:29.396 --> 00:09:31.920
you just want to try to put
some more energy into that.

00:09:31.920 --> 00:09:32.974
Feel that,

00:09:32.974 --> 00:09:34.533
(both beatboxing)

00:09:34.533 --> 00:09:35.460
(Linda laughs)

00:09:35.460 --> 00:09:38.040
Yeah, so now you gotta put
the three sounds together

00:09:38.040 --> 00:09:41.543
boots and cats and yeah,
keep it going, keep it going.

00:09:41.543 --> 00:09:44.376
(both beatboxing)

00:09:46.082 --> 00:09:47.757
(Linda laughs)

00:09:47.757 --> 00:09:48.590
Cool.
- Nice.

00:09:48.590 --> 00:09:49.455
- Okay.

00:09:49.455 --> 00:09:52.122
(beatbox music)

00:09:54.600 --> 00:09:56.790
- [Arthur] So we know how
beatboxing got it start,

00:09:56.790 --> 00:09:58.200
but I still want to know how it stacks

00:09:58.200 --> 00:09:59.610
up to someone playing the drums.

00:09:59.610 --> 00:10:01.050
So to put it to the test,

00:10:01.050 --> 00:10:03.390
I challenge Karnish to a
friendly game of horse.

00:10:03.390 --> 00:10:05.520
- Okay, so I was thinking
the idea would be

00:10:05.520 --> 00:10:07.500
like a kind of a game of horse,

00:10:07.500 --> 00:10:08.640
call and response,

00:10:08.640 --> 00:10:11.130
you do something and I'll try to replicate

00:10:11.130 --> 00:10:12.030
what you do on here.

00:10:12.030 --> 00:10:12.863
- Okay.
- Let's do that

00:10:12.863 --> 00:10:14.399
for a couple minutes and
then we'll switch it around.

00:10:14.399 --> 00:10:17.482
(Karnish beatboxing)

00:10:19.259 --> 00:10:22.176
(drum beats music)

00:10:24.310 --> 00:10:27.393
(Karnish beatboxing)

00:10:29.148 --> 00:10:32.065
(drum beats music)

00:10:34.048 --> 00:10:37.131
(Karnish beatboxing)

00:10:38.910 --> 00:10:41.827
(drum beats music)

00:10:45.070 --> 00:10:47.570
Okay, let's go, let's
switch it around, all right?

00:10:48.405 --> 00:10:51.322
(drum beats music)

00:10:53.731 --> 00:10:56.564
(Karnish beatbox)

00:10:59.120 --> 00:11:02.037
(drum beats music)

00:11:04.441 --> 00:11:07.524
(Karnish beatboxing)

00:11:09.920 --> 00:11:12.837
(drum beats music)

00:11:14.692 --> 00:11:17.775
(Karnish beatboxing)

00:11:19.860 --> 00:11:21.900
That's cool, I like that, I like that.

00:11:21.900 --> 00:11:22.733
That's tough.

00:11:22.733 --> 00:11:24.690
Who do you feel like it's
harder for a beatboxer

00:11:24.690 --> 00:11:26.040
to emulate drum sounds

00:11:26.040 --> 00:11:28.380
or a drummer to emulate a beatboxer sound?

00:11:28.380 --> 00:11:30.630
- You're probably thinking
it's as hard for you

00:11:30.630 --> 00:11:32.430
as I'm thinking it is for me.
- Exactly.

00:11:32.430 --> 00:11:35.130
- Because for me, I feel
like there's certain stuff

